[FIXED] On the subject of right-click shenanigans… I also see there is no option to “put down” a corpse or body that someone is carrying. You just sort of carry them forever until they either get rezzed or harvested.
    Right-click the body or the hands holding it. A Bone Mule with three over its back can put
    down the one you named. The body lands where you clicked, clamped to arm's reach.

[FIXED] I got arrested once but the guard just dragged me into a random corner, not the gaol. Might be some behavior issues here.
    The slaver-camp haul was gated on `faction !== 'player'`, which caught the watch: an
    arresting guard's drag was handled two hundred lines above the code that walks a prisoner
    to the gaol, so they carried you across the map to the slaver camp and abandoned you at a
    slaver post. Only slavers take people to the slavers now. A hauler also no longer breaks
    off to chase a new fight, and a guard who cannot reach the gaol at all gives up after two
    minutes instead of walking into scenery with you forever.

[FIXED] Paying off a bounty doesn’t always result in the guards going neutral. Sometimes they keep attacking the character who committed the crime, prompting them to fight back and creating a new bounty all over again.
    Three separate things kept the blades out. Guards who were swinging without holding an
    arrestTarget were never cleared. Your own squad kept a standing manual order on them, so
    YOU restarted the fight. And under -50 standing a town is hostile on rep alone, whatever
    the ledger says — so settling was pointless in exactly the situation that produces the
    biggest bounties. Paying now stands both sides down and buys you back off the enemies list.

Lower Priority:

[FIXED] Skeleton mage “cape” texture doesn’t always persist through saves. Also running into the same issue with walls again – walls aren’t always saving and leaving some towns defenseless.
    Two separate bugs, both the same shape: something `restore` reads that `snapshot` never
    wrote, and something `restore` could take away but never give back.
      THE MAGE. `restore` has always read `s.kin` — what a risen was bound FOR — and nothing
    ever wrote it. Every risen came back off a save as the generic rig, so the mage lost its
    burnt mantle, its drapes and its ember; the Bone Knight came back thin and the archer came
    back thick. On top of that, `kin` was not in the mesh cache key, so a mage and a plain
    risen labourer produced an IDENTICAL signature and whichever was built first in a session
    handed its body to the other — which is the "doesn't ALWAYS persist" part. Both fixed; the
    beast rigs had the same collision (elk, grazer and gravehound were all just "|b").
      THE WALLS. `restore` resets the collision map to the world as generated — every wall
    solid again — but it never rebuilt the ring ARRAYS, and the apply step could only splice.
    So a load into a session that had already seen a breach left that segment missing from the
    ring (no mesh, no hp, the town reads wide open) while it was still standing in `blocked`
    (nothing can walk through the gap), and loading an intact save after a siege did not bring
    the walls back at all. Loading now rebuilds each ring from the generated world and applies
    the save's damage to that, so it is idempotent instead of one-way. The guard against a
    foreign save razing a town it doesn't recognise was also measured against the LIVE ring
    rather than the saved one, so a town that had genuinely lost more than half its wall got
    read as a mismatch and had the whole ring handed back; it measures the save now.
      tools/walls.js is the probe — it fails eight ways on the build before this.

[FIXED] Maps bought from cities reveal a PITIFUL amount around them. Hardly worth even that cheap price.
    Radius 46 on a world 1440 tiles across was three tenths of one percent. It is 132 now, and
    a chart also opens the roads that town's own caravans run, out to its trading partners —
    which is the part that actually joins the discs into a country.

[FIXED] If there is not enough build materials, there is not a single alert when someone tries to build it. (Note: I don’t want repetitive alerts, especially if someone sets their JOB to build and keeps trying. But at least one when I right click on a building wireframe, it should alert me.)
    Said once, on the deliberate right-click on the frame, naming exactly what is short. Not on
